MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Well, it will you whta you want, see also http://shiny.rstudio.com/tutorial/lesson6/

  lastA <- reactive({
     input$a
     Sys.time()
  })

  lastB <- reactive({
     input$b
     Sys.time()
  })

  output$my.table <- renderDataTable({
     lasta <- lastA()
     lastb <- lastB()
     if (lasta<lastb) { # check how time comparisons can be done R correctly
       # b was changed last
     } elseif (lasta>lastb) {
       # a was changed last
     }
   })

Shiny knows that my.table depends on input$a and input$b. If either of them is changed then renderDataTable must be run. However, if input$a is changed then lastA() will be run, it may deliver a new result. Since input$b has not been changed shiny does not run lastB() again (it is a reactive expression!), but it delivers the (stored) result from the last time when lastB() has been really run; which actually is the last change of input$b. As result in both routines deliver the last time when input$a and input$b has changed such that you can easily compare them.

I guess that is what you want. Best Sigbert

